# Healthcare Data Generator — environment variables
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.
# .env is gitignored and will NOT be committed.
#
# ── Quick Start ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. cp .env.example .env
# 2. Set CONNECTION_STRING below (URL-encoded ODBC string)
# 3. pip install -r requirements.txt
# 4. python src/main.py --export-parquet          (no DB needed)
#    python src/main.py --rebuild-start 2025-01-01 (writes to DB)
#
# ── Database Connection ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Provide a **URL-encoded** ODBC connection string.
# The app builds the SQLAlchemy URL as:
#   mssql+pyodbc:///?odbc_connect=<CONNECTION_STRING>
#
# Example — Azure SQL / Fabric SQL with Azure AD Interactive auth:
#   CONNECTION_STRING=Driver%3D%7BODBC+Driver+18+for+SQL+Server%7D%3BServer%3Dyour-server.database.fabric.microsoft.com%2C1433%3BDatabase%3Dyour-database%3BEncrypt%3Dyes%3BTrustServerCertificate%3Dno%3BAuthentication%3DActiveDirectoryInteractive
#
# Example — SQL Server with username/password:
#   CONNECTION_STRING=Driver%3D%7BODBC+Driver+18+for+SQL+Server%7D%3BServer%3Dlocalhost%3BDatabase%3Dhealthcare%3BUID%3Dsa%3BPWD%3DYourPassword%3BEncrypt%3Dno
#
# Tip: URL-encode a raw ODBC string with Python:
#   python -c "import urllib.parse; print(urllib.parse.quote_plus('Driver={ODBC Driver 18 for SQL Server};Server=...'))"
#
# NOTE: CONNECTION_STRING is only required for database modes.
#       --export-parquet works without any connection string.
CONNECTION_STRING=
